[17592186134228 1071 "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534321 17592186045417 true] [17592186134228 1074 "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nNow, a vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot {h}_{j}$ for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$  \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =2\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 4=9\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{1}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 3+(-4)\\cdot 4=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 2=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 4=1\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{2}$ only\"." 13194139534321 17592186045417 true] [17592186418736 1043 #edb/ref 17592186418770 13194139534321 17592186045417 true] [17592186418736 1043 #edb/ref 17592186418771 13194139534321 17592186045417 true] [17592186418736 1043 #edb/ref 17592186418772 13194139534321 17592186045417 true] [17592186418736 1043 #edb/ref 17592186418773 13194139534321 17592186045417 true] [17592186418736 1043 #edb/ref 17592186418774 13194139534321 17592186045417 true] [17592186418736 1044 #ed